Carrion-Roll

Ingredients: HARD ROCK + LIQUID FUNK + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Fiddle  
Creative Catalyst: It emerged from a desert caravan culture that trades in salvaged synthesizers.

Carrion-Roll is an aggressive, atmospheric collision where the breakneck, fluid syncopation of 172 BPM liquid funk breakbeats collides with down-tuned hard rock riffing, cavernous gothic melodrama, and raw, rosin-burned fiddle leads. Drifting, sun-damaged polyphonic synth textures ride underneath like heat mirages, producing a sound that is simultaneously feral, danceable, and deeply haunted.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 170–174 BPM. A relentless, rolling liquid two-step break with hyper-kinetic ghost snares, anchored by the heavy, swung downbeats of drop-D rock power chords and deep sub-bass glides.
- Harmonic Palette: Rooted in D minor and B Phrygian dominant; dominated by flattened fifths, minor second dissonances, and mournful modal drones derived from Appalachian fiddle ballads and Balkan lamentations.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Rosin-Overdrive Tracking: Close-miking the fiddle with ribbon microphones, then splitting the signal through both an antique tape echo and a high-gain tube guitar amplifier to create a jagged, weeping bite.
  2. Oxide-Choked Breaks: Slicing classic drum breaks through dusty, desert-salvaged 12-bit hardware samplers, crushing high frequencies with analog grit while keeping the 40 Hz sub-bass completely clean and fluid.
  3. Uncalibrated Oscillator Stabs: Using salvaged analog synths with temperature-drifted voice boards to play sinister chord stabs that subtly beat against the microtonal bends of the live fiddle.
- Dynamics: Tracks open in hushed, menacing stillness—solitary fiddle screeches, tape hiss, and detuned synth drones—before abruptly detonating into a high-speed, rolling assault of drums and sludge-heavy guitars, periodically dropping out into cavernous sub-bass valleys.

Scene & Origin Lore

Carrion-Roll crystallized along the scrub-choked highway corridors of the Sonoran and Mojave borders, where migratory scrap caravans trade in decommissioned telecommunications gear and discarded analog instruments dragged from shuttered military test sites and forgotten storage auctions. At dusk, generator-powered flatbed trailers are lashed together into makeshift amphitheaters under heavy tarps; the crowd gathers in grease-stained heavy denim, salt-bleached duster coats, and welder’s goggles to ward off the alkaline wind. Between sets, barter stalls sell hand-soldered synth voices wrapped in wax paper alongside bottles of horsehair rosin and moonshine. The scene's core ritual is the "Demag Circle," where fiddlers and breakbeat programmers jam until the diesel generators seize from engine heat, creating an intense, communal catharsis where rural gothic string traditions and salvaged British drum-and-bass tapes bleed into desert rock grit.

Typical Instruments: 
- Acoustic and amplified fiddle (run through fuzz boxes and spring reverb tanks)
- Drop-tuned electric baritone guitars (sludge and hard rock crunch)
- Sliced, rolling drum breaks (processed via vintage Akai and E-mu samplers)
- Salvaged analog synthesizers (Korg MS-20, Roland Juno-60 with drifting voices, circuit-bent Casio CZs)
- Moog Taurus-style sub-bass pedals and sliding 808 subs

For Fans Of: The Prodigy, Calibre, Fields of the Nephilim, Dirty Three

