Track 3: Sister Vael at the Demag Wells
- Story & Context: Local folklore insists that Sister Vael composed this sprawling nine-minute dirge after discovering a pit of decommissioned bulk tape erasers left rotting near mile marker 40 in the Mojave scrub. Using the residual magnetic fields of the dead demagnetizers to distort her salvaged synthesizer's output, she layered the resulting low-frequency rumble beneath a frantic, galloping drum-and-bass rhythm and an unhinged, double-stopped fiddle solo. Legend says the band played the track continuously for twelve hours without food or water until their primary power inverter melted, closing the piece with a distorted, ghostly choir chanting, "Nothing sleeps beneath the salt." The recording ends abruptly when the magnetic tape itself tore from its spool, leaving behind a legendary local relic that every caravan DJ attempts to recreate.
